WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.038
Almost every part has holes: for bolts, pins, oil or cables.

2
00:00:06.589 --> 00:00:08.888
The standard tool is the twist drill.

3
00:00:09.438 --> 00:00:15.083
But start it on a flat block, and it slides a fraction sideways before it bites.

4
00:00:15.633 --> 00:00:17.479
That is called drill wander.

5
00:00:18.379 --> 00:00:22.129
So first you make a short dimple with a centre drill: a short,

6
00:00:22.329 --> 00:00:24.338
stiff drill with a broad point.

7
00:00:24.888 --> 00:00:28.650
In it, the twist drill finds its place straight away.

8
00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:33.439
Then the twist drill itself: two cutting edges at the point,

9
00:00:33.639 --> 00:00:36.298
and two grooves that spiral up the body.

10
00:00:36.848 --> 00:00:39.843
Along each flute the chips turn their way out.

11
00:00:40.393 --> 00:00:47.256
An 8 millimetre drill in high-speed steel runs at about 25 metres per minute:

12
00:00:47.256 --> 00:00:49.484
995 revolutions a minute.

13
00:00:50.684 --> 00:00:52.762
The point of the drill is a cone.

14
00:00:53.312 --> 00:00:56.601
The angle of that cone is the point angle:

15
00:00:56.601 --> 00:01:00.359
118 degrees on ordinary high-speed steel drills.

16
00:01:00.909 --> 00:01:03.277
That point centres the drill in the hole.

17
00:01:04.177 --> 00:01:09.045
This hole does not go through: it is a blind hole, with a cone-shaped bottom.

18
00:01:09.594 --> 00:01:13.147
20 millimetres deep takes about 8 seconds a hole.

19
00:01:14.347 --> 00:01:17.856
If it must be tighter, a second tool follows.

20
00:01:18.406 --> 00:01:23.096
For a 10 millimetre hole in H7 you first drill 9.8.

21
00:01:23.646 --> 00:01:27.501
A reamer then takes off the last 0.2 millimetres.

22
00:01:28.401 --> 00:01:31.571
The H says the hole may never be smaller than 10.

23
00:01:32.121 --> 00:01:35.309
The 7 says how wide the band is:

24
00:01:35.309 --> 00:01:40.887
the hole measures between 10.000 and 10.015 millimetres.

25
00:01:41.787 --> 00:01:47.119
Hole and pin together are called the fit: this pin slides in, a clearance fit.

26
00:01:48.319 --> 00:01:51.358
For a thread you first drill the tap drill hole:

27
00:01:51.358 --> 00:01:53.764
the size of the screw minus the pitch,

28
00:01:53.964 --> 00:01:56.147
the distance between two threads.

29
00:01:56.697 --> 00:02:02.865
For M8, with a pitch of 1.25 millimetres, that is a 6.8 drill.

30
00:02:03.765 --> 00:02:06.075
Then the tap cuts the thread: tapping.

31
00:02:06.625 --> 00:02:12.238
Taps break easily, above all in a blind hole, where the chips cannot get away.

32
00:02:13.138 --> 00:02:17.506
A large fitted hole, such as 40 H7 for a bearing,

33
00:02:17.706 --> 00:02:20.341
is opened up by a boring head: boring.

34
00:02:20.891 --> 00:02:25.372
The band here is 0 to 0.025 millimetres.

35
00:02:26.572 --> 00:02:31.160
In deep holes, what counts is the depth divided by the diameter.

36
00:02:31.711 --> 00:02:37.216
A hole of 6 millimetres, 90 deep, is 15 times the diameter.

37
00:02:37.766 --> 00:02:42.541
Then every flute fills up, and it is all about chip evacuation.

38
00:02:43.441 --> 00:02:48.413
In peck drilling the drill goes in a little, pulls back to clear the chips,

39
00:02:48.613 --> 00:02:49.716
and goes on.

40
00:02:50.266 --> 00:02:55.804
54 seconds of cutting plus 30 seconds of pulling back: 84 seconds a hole.

41
00:02:56.704 --> 00:03:01.292
With through-spindle coolant, coolant sprays through the drill at high pressure,

42
00:03:01.493 --> 00:03:03.409
and flushes the chips out.

43
00:03:03.958 --> 00:03:06.884
No pecking: 13.5 seconds.

44
00:03:07.434 --> 00:03:12.789
At 10,000 parts, that saves 196 hours a year.

45
00:03:13.989 --> 00:03:16.218
Deeper still, or extremely straight?

46
00:03:16.768 --> 00:03:21.331
Then it is a gun drill: a drill with one cutting edge and a hollow shank.

47
00:03:22.231 --> 00:03:27.447
The coolant runs through the shank at high pressure, and carries the chips back out,

48
00:03:27.647 --> 00:03:29.644
along a groove on the outside.

49
00:03:30.844 --> 00:03:34.768
A hole can have the right size and still be in the wrong place.

50
00:03:35.318 --> 00:03:38.323
That is what the position tolerance is for:

51
00:03:38.323 --> 00:03:42.516
the hole’s axis must lie within a circle of 0.1 millimetres.

52
00:03:43.416 --> 00:03:49.219
That circle sits round the exact spot set by the theoretically exact dimensions:

53
00:03:49.219 --> 00:03:50.742
sizes in a small box,

54
00:03:50.942 --> 00:03:52.916
with no tolerance of their own.

55
00:03:53.816 --> 00:04:02.631
The measuring machine measures hole 3: 0.024 millimetres off in X, and 0.032 in Y.

56
00:04:03.181 --> 00:04:06.757
That is 0.04 from the exact spot.

57
00:04:07.306 --> 00:04:13.671
Because the tolerance is a diameter, the position is 2 times that: 0.08.

58
00:04:14.871 --> 00:04:18.566
Perpendicularity says whether the hole stands straight:

59
00:04:18.566 --> 00:04:20.850
its axis must be square to face A,

60
00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:24.939
within 0.05 millimetres over the depth.

61
00:04:25.839 --> 00:04:29.260
In series the part goes onto a functional gauge:

62
00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:31.969
a plate with pins at the exact places.

63
00:04:32.869 --> 00:04:36.006
If the part fits, the position is good.

64
00:04:36.556 --> 00:04:38.483
Quick, and hard to get wrong.
